## Background: Why Approach A over Approach B

### Approach A (Recommended): Reuse DETECT/SEGMENT paths with configurable metadata

**How it works for YOLOE:**
1. User exports YOLOE model **with fuse()** — this bakes text embeddings into the model weights
2. The fused ONNX model has standard YOLO output format: [1, 300, 6] (detect) or [1, 300, 38] + [1, 32, 160, 160] (seg)
3. User provides a model_name.yaml alongside the .onnx with classes: and optional input_size:
4. No new TaskType, no new inference code path, no text encoder needed in C++

**Justification:**
- **YOLOE ONNX output after fuse() is IDENTICAL to standard YOLO.** The head difference (YOLOEDetect vs Detect) is resolved at export time. The exported graph has the same [1, 300, 6] output tensor. The SDK preprocessing (letterbox + blobFromImage) and postprocessing (bbox decode + letterbox reversal) apply unchanged.
- **Zero new inference code required.** Every line of infer(), postprocess(), and postprocessSeg() works without modification.
- **Fixes the root problem, not just YOLOE.** The hardcoded COCO class list blocks ALL custom models (industrial inspection with scratch/dent/crack, medical imaging, robotics). Making this configurable is the No.1 blocker for production use regardless of YOLOE.
- **Text prompt flexibility is preserved through model selection.** For open-vocabulary use, the user exports multiple fused YOLOE models with different vocabularies. The SDK loads whichever model is needed. This is actually more practical than runtime text prompts for most industrial deployments where vocabularies are domain-fixed.
- **Approach B is premature.** Text prompt support requires: (a) CLIP tokenizer in C++ or Python pre-processing, (b) dual-input ONNX models (image + text embeddings), (c) new IInferenceEngine API surface, (d) new pybind11 bindings. This adds ~500+ lines of complex C++ for a feature that can be satisfied by fused models today, and would only benefit interactive/exploratory use cases.

### When Approach B would make sense (future work, out of scope)
- Interactive applications where the user types arbitrary categories at runtime
- When the YOLOE model must be shared across many users with different vocabularies
- When text embeddings need to change per-frame

